findings:
Implemented and green: npm run build clean, npm test 327/327 (no regressions; the dashboard is plain JS with no unit suite, consistent with the repo). All six acceptance criteria met.

DASHBOARD (public/, vanilla ESM, no bundler):
- api.js: added agent methods (listAgents, getAgent, agentProcesses, agentMetrics, agentLogs, agentControl, setAlias) and a processBase(agentId) helper. Existing process methods (listProcesses/getProcess/metrics/logs/control/deleteProcess/errors) now take an OPTIONAL trailing agentId that selects the agent-scoped base path. Standalone call sites pass no agentId -> byte-identical requests. NOTE: the agent surface has NO single-process GET, so deleteProcess(name, agentId) maps to POST /api/agents/:id/processes/:name/delete (routed control) rather than the DELETE verb, and detail metadata is pulled from the agent process LIST (see detail.js fetchProcess).
- ws.js: logSubscribe/logUnsubscribe take an optional agentId; the subscription map is keyed by `${agentId}/${process}` in server mode (plain process in standalone) so the same name on two agents does not collide; resubscribe preserves agentId. With no agentId the frame is byte-identical ({type,process,streams}); agentId is only added to the frame when present.
- views/agents.js (NEW): fleet overview — agent cards (alias-or-id, online badge, host/platform from meta, process count, pm2-connected badge) with inline alias edit (PUT /api/agents/:id/alias, Enter/Escape/Save/Cancel) showing the real id on hover + in the edit field (AC-37). Selecting a card drills into the per-agent overview.
- views/overview.js + detail.js + logs.js: each gained an optional agentId threaded into every api.* / ws.* call; the SAME components render per-agent via only a base-path change. detail.js#fetchProcess uses api.agentProcesses(id).find(name) in server mode (no single-process agent GET). Standalone paths unchanged.
- app.js: discovers mode from GET /api/system/health; mode defaults to 'standalone' on any probe failure so standalone never changes. Server mode adds a Fleet nav link + AgentsView, hides the local-PM2 banner, ignores the local-state WS snapshot for the grid (per-agent grids load via REST), and rebuilds overview/detail bound to the selected agentId. Alert toasts show [alias-or-id] when agentId is present. Standalone wiring is behaviorally identical to before.
- index.html: added a hidden <section id="view-fleet">; css: a small .agent-alias-input rule. The dashboard shell (index.html) is byte-identical across modes (verified in the smoke).

SERVER STATIC MOUNT (src/boot/server.ts): buildServerApp did NOT serve public/ — the fleet dashboard would never load in server mode. Added `app.use('/', express.static(PUBLIC_DIR))` (same PUBLIC_DIR resolution as standalone: dirname/../../public), mounted unauthenticated after the /api routers and before the /api 404, exactly mirroring standalone's static mount. This is the one source edit in FEAT-005; all 327 tests still pass.

CONFIG/DOCS:
- .gitignore: added config/agent-id and config/agent-aliases.json.
- ecosystem.config.cjs: documented MODE + the agent vars (SERVER_URL/AGENT_TOKEN/AGENT_NAME/AGENT_ID_FILE/AGENT_WS_PATH/TLS_INSECURE) and server vars (AGENT_TOKENS/ALIAS_STORE_FILE/AGENT_WS_PATH/TLS_CERT_FILE/TLS_KEY_FILE) as commented examples grouped by mode.
- .env.example + README.md: documented all three run modes with an example per mode, the agent identity/alias model (AGENT_NAME seeds, operator alias wins, real id on hover), the versioned JSON discriminated-union protocol summary, token rotation/revocation + separate credential spaces + constant-time comparison, TLS verify-by-default + the insecure toggle + reverse-proxy vs native TLS, the alias persistence file, the new /api/agents REST endpoints (with the fleet 409/404 status additions), and the server-mode live-ring /logs semantics vs the standalone file tail. README config table gained all new vars.
- IMPORTANT doc fix found during the smoke: SERVER_URL is the BASE host and AGENT_WS_PATH is APPENDED (joinUrl). The original .env.example/README/ecosystem examples showed `SERVER_URL=wss://host/agent` which would double to `/agent/agent` and never connect. Corrected all three to show the base host only, with an explicit note that AGENT_WS_PATH is appended.

END-TO-END HUB-AND-SPOKE SMOKE (design §9): a real local PM2 daemon WAS available (pm2 5.4.3), so the full scripted smoke ran (not the FEAT-004 integration-test substitute). Script + demo process kept under .agents/tasks/multi-instance/ (smoke.mjs, smoke-demo.cjs); it boots MODE=server (token, native TLS off, plain ws) + MODE=agent against the local PM2 with a demo process, over loopback. Result: SMOKE GREEN — 9/9 checks:
  1. server health reports mode=server (agents summary, no pm2Connected);
  2. the agent appears online in GET /api/agents (stable id <hostname>-<suffix>, nameHint seeded as the alias);
  3. the demo process is visible via /api/agents/:id/processes;
  4. live logs stream in the drill-down over the human WS relay (log:subscribe with agentId -> relayed log frames carrying agentId);
  5. a control command round-trips (POST /api/agents/:id/processes/:name/restart -> ok:true with the fresh snapshot);
  6. an alias set is accepted (PUT /api/agents/:id/alias);
  7. the alias PERSISTS across a fleet restart (written to ALIAS_STORE_FILE, reloaded on server boot, re-attached to the reconnected agent keyed by the persisted AGENT_ID_FILE suffix);
  8. MODE unset still serves the standalone dashboard with health mode:'standalone' + pm2Connected (byte-identical shell);
  9. GET / returns the identical index.html (2677B) in both server and standalone modes.
To verify locally: `env -u ALERT_RULES_FILE -u AUTH_MODE -u API_KEY -u MODE NODE_PATH=<repo>/node_modules node .agents/tasks/multi-instance/smoke.mjs` (this worktree shares the main checkout's node_modules; agent mode needs a dummy API_KEY only because the config schema still requires human-auth config in all modes).

OBSERVATION (NOT a FEAT-005 defect; flagged for FEAT-002 follow-up): when ONLY the server bounces via SIGTERM (graceful) and the agent must self-heal, the agent did not reliably reconnect within ~70s in manual testing — the transport close/dead-link reconnect loop appears to stall after the first ECONNREFUSED retry. A SIGKILL server crash is detected and retried correctly, and a full fleet restart (both processes) reconnects fine, which is why the smoke exercises alias persistence via a fleet restart. This is in the agent-connection reconnect machinery (FEAT-002/connection.ts), outside FEAT-005's dashboard/docs/config scope; the FEAT-004 in-process integration test (AC-59) covers the handshake/control/log round-trip as the automated substitute. Recommend a FEAT-002 follow-up to make the server-only-restart reconnect robust.
